    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Growing up in a fundamental Muslim family, my exposure to socio-political discourse and narratives of conflict from a young age cultivated a deep awareness of geopolitical tensions and the harsh realities faced by individuals caught in the crosshairs of violence. Through these experiences, I gained valuable insights into the complexities of reconciling divergent viewpoints and the challenges inherent in fostering genuine dialogue across profound divides in the pursuit of democracy.  \r\n\r\nWhile there have been a lot of efforts to facilitate political dialogue and engagement across sectors, many studies nevertheless report concerning trends of decreased civility and increased polarization in the U.S. and abroad. It is essential to consider their collective effectiveness in light of these broader societal trends. In response to these challenges, I initiated a project aimed at delving deeper into the influences shaping political discourse. This initiative was hosted by a non-profit organization I co-founded called the Democracy Institute for Civic Engagement.\r\n By conducting interviews with leading scholars and individuals engaged in socio-political discourse, and drawing upon diverse academic disciplines, I developed a conceptual framework to analyze the components that constitute democratic discourse. This framework acknowledges the inherent messiness and complexities of political discourse, spanning individual, institutional, and global scales. It is divided into four interconnected dimensions: the political dimension, which explores power dynamics and competing interests; the technical dimension, focused on practical skills and tools for constructive dialogue; the individual dimension, examining human psychology and personal behaviors; and the systemic dimension, which addresses underlying structural and institutional factors. This framework aims to provide a holistic understanding of the current state of political discourse and the factors that shape it. Ultimately, this analysis emphasizes the need for a multifaceted approach that addresses individual capacities for dialogue, institutional policies, and systemic influences – a comprehensive strategy that acknowledges the many pieces that contribute to the support of democratic processes and engagement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
